  "clips": [
    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "fork, exec, wait",
      "question": "What does fork return, and why does a shell fork and then exec?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Processes, question one. What does fork return? And why does a shell call fork, and then exec, instead of one call that starts a program? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What does <em>fork</em> return, and why <em>fork</em> then <em>exec</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Fork creates a near copy of the calling process, and both copies return from it. The return value tells them apart: zero in the child, and the child's process I D in the parent. Negative one means it failed. Before running anything, the child can rearrange its own state. Here it points standard output at a file: that's how a shell does redirection. Then exec replaces the running program with a new one: same process, same I D, new code. It only returns if it fails. Meanwhile, the parent calls wait pid, which blocks until the child exits, and collects its status.",
          "props": {
            "title": "A tiny <em>shell</em>",
            "file": "shell.c",
            "tag": "process API",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 20,
            "width": 1210,
            "code": "while (read_command(line, &argv, &outfile)) {\n    pid_t pid = fork();                    // one process becomes two\n    if (pid < 0) { perror(\"fork\"); continue; }\n    if (pid == 0) {                        // child: fork returned 0\n        if (outfile) {                     // \"cmd > out.txt\"\n            int fd = open(outfile, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);\n            dup2(fd, STDOUT_FILENO);       // stdout now writes to the file\n            close(fd);\n        }\n        execvp(argv[0], argv);             // replace the program; no return\n        perror(\"execvp\");                  // ...unless it failed\n        _exit(127);\n    }\n    int status;                            // parent: fork returned the child's PID\n    waitpid(pid, &status, 0);              // wait for it, and reap it\n}",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "copy",
                "lines": "2",
                "note": "One process becomes two"
              },
              {
                "at": "zero",
                "lines": "4",
                "note": "Child: fork returned 0"
              },
              {
                "at": "standard",
                "lines": "5-8",
                "note": "Set up the child first: redirect stdout"
              },
              {
                "at": "replaces",
                "lines": "10",
                "note": "Same PID, new program"
              },
              {
                "at": "fails",
                "lines": "11-12",
                "note": "Only returns on error"
              },
              {
                "at": "blocks",
                "lines": "14-15",
                "note": "Parent: wait for the child, and reap it"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "States and zombies",
      "question": "What is a zombie process, and how do you get rid of one?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. What is a zombie process? And how do you get rid of one? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What is a <em>zombie</em>, and how do you get rid of one?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "A process is running on a CPU, ready and waiting for one, or blocked on some event. The scheduler moves a ready process onto a CPU. A timer interrupt can preempt it, back to ready. If it starts disk or network I/O, it blocks, and when the I/O completes, it's ready again. When it exits, the kernel frees its memory and closes its files. But its entry in the process table stays, holding the exit status, until the parent collects it. Until then it's a zombie, shown as Z in ps. Once the parent calls wait, the entry is gone.",
