    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Copy-on-write fork",
      "question": "fork copies the whole address space. So why is it fast?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. Fork gives the child a copy of the parent's whole address space, maybe gigabytes. So why is fork fast? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "fork copies the whole address space. Why is it <em>fast</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Copying gigabytes on every fork would be slow, and wasteful, because most children call exec right away. So fork copies only the page tables, and points both processes at the same physical frames, marked read only. Reads are shared freely. When either process writes, the CPU raises a page fault. The kernel sees a copy on write page, copies just that page into a new frame, maps it writable for the writer, and retries the write. A page nobody writes is never copied.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Copy on <em>write</em>",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "ppt",
                "label": "parent\npage table",
                "x": 420,
                "y": 300,
                "w": 280,
                "h": 96
              },
              {
                "id": "cpt",
                "label": "child\npage table",
