        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "So fork, then exec, is nearly free: exec throws the copied page tables away. But a big process that keeps writing pays per page. Redis's background save forks a child to write a snapshot. Every page the parent writes during the save gets copied, so memory can nearly double. And fork still copies page tables, about two megabytes per gigabyte, so spawning helpers from a huge process is better done with posix spawn.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Cheap, <em>not free</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "exec",
              "label": "fork + exec",
              "title": "Nearly free",
              "tone": "good",
              "body": "Only page tables are copied, and exec discards them."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "snapshot",
              "label": "fork of a busy heap",
              "title": "One copy per written page",
              "body": "Redis BGSAVE: pages the parent writes get copied; memory can nearly double."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "megabytes",
              "text": "fork still copies page tables: ~2 MB per GB of memory.",
              "code": "posix_spawn"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. Fork returns zero in the child, and the child's P I D in the parent. Exec replaces the program, keeping the process. A zombie is an exited child nobody has waited for. And copy on write makes fork cheap, until someone writes.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Processes, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "returns",
                "text": "fork: 0 in the child, the child's PID in the parent.",
                "code": "pid_t pid = fork()"
